<?php

// Curated Vault AI Interview questions: C# & .NET (stack key csharp_dotnet, role backend). 3 series x 3 sets x 5 questions.
// Set ids are {role}-{stack}-s{series}-set{set}. Loaded by database/seeders/CuratedSetQuestionsSeeder (glob) and by the
// 2026_11_19_100000 migration, both as idempotent upserts.

$t = static fn (string $text, string $note, string $category = 'technical'): array => [
    'text' => $text, 'category' => $category, 'duration_seconds' => 180, 'note' => $note,
];

return [

    // ───────────────────────── Series 1 — foundations ─────────────────────────

    'backend-csharp_dotnet-s1-set1' => [
        $t('Explain value types versus reference types in C#. Where do they live in memory, how does assignment behave for each, and when would you choose a struct over a class?',
            'Value types (struct, enum, primitives) copy their data on assignment and are usually stored inline (stack or inside the containing object); reference types hold a reference to a heap object, so assignment copies the reference. Structs suit small, immutable, short-lived data (under about 16 bytes); large or mutable structs cause copying costs and surprising bugs. Mention boxing when a value type is treated as object.'),
        $t('Strings in C# are immutable. What does that mean in practice, why is repeated concatenation in a loop a problem, and when do you reach for StringBuilder?',
            'Every concatenation creates a new string object, so a loop of N appends is O(N squared) in copying and allocates garbage. StringBuilder keeps a mutable buffer and is the right tool for many appends; a handful of concatenations or string interpolation is fine. Mention string interning and that immutability makes strings safe to share across threads.'),
        $t('Compare an abstract class with an interface. Since C# 8 interfaces can have default implementations, so when do you still prefer an abstract class?',
            'An abstract class can hold state (fields), constructors and protected members and gives a shared base with partial implementation, but a class has only one base. Interfaces define a contract that many unrelated types implement; default interface methods help evolve an interface without breaking implementers but cannot hold instance state. Prefer abstract classes for a real is-a hierarchy with shared state, interfaces for capabilities and for testability.'),
        $t('What is the difference between ==, Equals() and ReferenceEquals() in C#? What must you do if you override Equals, and how do records change this?',
            'ReferenceEquals compares object identity; == is overloadable (reference equality by default for classes, value equality for strings and many structs); Equals is the virtual equality method. If you override Equals you must override GetHashCode consistently (equal objects must have equal hashes) or hash-based collections break. Records generate value-based Equals, GetHashCode and == over their members.'),
        $t('What are nullable reference types in C# 8 and later? How do the ? and ! operators, ?. and ?? help, and why do they not remove NullReferenceException completely?',
            'Nullable reference types add compile-time flow analysis: string is non-nullable by intent, string? may be null, and the compiler warns on possible null dereference. ?. short-circuits on null, ?? supplies a default, ! suppresses a warning (a promise by the developer). It is only static analysis with warnings, so legacy code, reflection, deserialisation or a misused ! can still throw at runtime.'),
    ],

    'backend-csharp_dotnet-s1-set2' => [
        $t('When would you use an array, List<T>, Dictionary<TKey,TValue>, HashSet<T> or Queue<T>? Give the typical time complexity of lookup and insert for each.',
            'Array: fixed size, O(1) index. List<T>: dynamic array, O(1) index, amortised O(1) append, O(n) insert or search. Dictionary: hash table, average O(1) lookup and insert by key. HashSet: average O(1) membership and uniqueness. Queue: O(1) enqueue and dequeue for FIFO work. Choose by access pattern, not habit; mention that bad GetHashCode degrades hash collections.'),
        $t('What is deferred execution in LINQ? Show how it can surprise you, for example when enumerating a query twice or modifying the source after defining the query.',
            'A LINQ query over IEnumerable is only a description until it is enumerated (foreach, ToList, Count, First). Each enumeration re-runs the pipeline, so side effects or expensive sources repeat and changes to the source between definition and enumeration are visible. Materialise with ToList or ToArray when you need a snapshot or to avoid multiple enumeration.'),
        $t('What is the difference between IEnumerable<T> and IQueryable<T>, and why does it matter when you query a database with Entity Framework?',
            'IEnumerable runs LINQ in memory using delegates; IQueryable builds an expression tree that the provider (EF Core) translates to SQL so filtering happens in the database. Calling AsEnumerable or ToList too early pulls the whole table and filters in memory. Keep the query as IQueryable until the filters, ordering and paging are applied.'),
        $t('Explain what async and await actually do. Does await create a new thread? How does Task differ from Thread?',
            'async methods are compiled into a state machine; await yields control to the caller while the awaited operation (usually I/O) is pending and resumes the method afterwards, without blocking a thread. It does not create a thread by itself. A Task represents a unit of asynchronous work or a future result, scheduled on the thread pool or completed by I/O callbacks, while Thread is an OS-level thread that is expensive to create.'),
        $t('What are delegates, events, Func<> and Action<>? How do lambda expressions capture variables, and what is a closure?',
            'A delegate is a type-safe function pointer; Func<> returns a value and Action<> returns void. An event is a delegate with restricted access (only the owner can raise it, others can only subscribe or unsubscribe). Lambdas capture variables, not values, so a captured loop variable or a long-lived subscription can keep objects alive or behave unexpectedly; the closure is the compiler-generated class holding captured variables.'),
    ],

    'backend-csharp_dotnet-s1-set3' => [
        $t('Explain the CLR, IL and the JIT compiler. How do .NET Framework, .NET Core, .NET Standard and modern .NET (5 and later) relate to each other?',
            'C# compiles to Intermediate Language plus metadata in an assembly; the CLR loads it and the JIT compiles IL to machine code at run time. .NET Framework is the Windows-only original; .NET Core is the cross-platform rewrite, unified as .NET 5 and later; .NET Standard was an API contract for sharing libraries across them and is largely replaced by targeting modern .NET. New work should target a supported LTS version.'),
        $t('How does the .NET garbage collector work? Explain generations, the Large Object Heap and the role of IDisposable and finalizers.',
            'The GC is generational (gen 0, 1, 2): most objects die young, so gen 0 is collected often and cheaply; objects of about 85,000 bytes or more go to the LOH, collected with gen 2. The GC frees managed memory only; unmanaged resources (files, sockets, handles) need IDisposable with using or Dispose, with a finalizer only as a safety net because finalizable objects survive extra collections.'),
        $t('What is the difference between throw; and throw ex; in a catch block? How should exceptions be designed and handled in a .NET application?',
            'throw; rethrows and preserves the original stack trace; throw ex; resets it, hiding where the error began. Catch only what you can handle, use specific exception types, use finally or using for cleanup, do not use exceptions for normal control flow (they are slow), and log with the full exception. Exception filters (when) allow conditional catching without unwinding.'),
        $t('Managed code has a garbage collector, yet applications still leak memory. Give common causes of memory leaks in .NET and how you would find them.',
            'Typical causes: event handlers or static events that keep subscribers alive, static collections or caches that grow without bounds, undisposed IDisposable objects, captured variables in long-lived closures, and timers. Find them with a memory profiler or dotnet-gcdump and dotnet-dump, comparing heap snapshots and following the retention path (GC roots) of the growing type.'),
        $t('How do you make shared state thread-safe in C#? Compare lock, Interlocked, ConcurrentDictionary and immutable data, and explain how a deadlock happens.',
            'lock gives mutual exclusion for a critical section; Interlocked offers atomic operations on single variables; concurrent collections handle concurrent access internally; immutable data avoids sharing mutable state at all. A deadlock occurs when threads hold locks and wait for each other in a cycle; prevent it with a consistent lock ordering, short critical sections, no awaits or callbacks inside locks, and timeouts.'),
    ],

    // ───────────────────────── Series 2 — applied depth ─────────────────────────

    'backend-csharp_dotnet-s2-set1' => [
        $t('Explain generic constraints (where T : class, struct, new(), an interface) and covariance and contravariance. Why is IEnumerable<out T> covariant but List<T> not?',
            'Constraints let generic code call members or create instances of T safely. Covariance (out) lets IEnumerable<Dog> be used as IEnumerable<Animal> because it only produces T; contravariance (in) lets Action<Animal> be used as Action<Dog> because it only consumes T. List<T> both reads and writes T, so allowing covariance would let you add a Cat to a list of Dogs and break type safety.'),
        $t('What do records, init-only properties, the with expression and pattern matching switch expressions give you? When would you choose a record over a class or struct?',
            'Records give concise immutable-friendly types with value equality, a readable ToString and non-destructive mutation using with; init-only setters allow object initialisers without later mutation; switch expressions with patterns (type, property, relational, list) express branching declaratively. Use records for DTOs, messages and value objects; classes for entities with identity and behaviour; structs for tiny value-like data.'),
        $t('A class implements two interfaces that declare the same method name. How do explicit interface implementations solve this, and what is the downside of default interface methods and multiple inheritance of behaviour?',
            'Explicit implementation (void IA.Do()) provides a separate body per interface and is reachable only through that interface type, resolving the ambiguity. Default interface methods can create diamond-like conflicts that the compiler forces you to resolve explicitly, and they hide logic in interfaces; keep them for backward-compatible interface evolution, not as a substitute for composition.'),
        $t('How do extension methods work and how does the compiler resolve them? What are their limitations and risks in a shared codebase?',
            'An extension method is a static method in a static class whose first parameter uses this; calls compile to a normal static call, so they cannot access private members or be overridden, and an instance method with the same signature always wins. Risks: name collisions across namespaces, discoverability through using directives, extending types you do not own in surprising ways, and null receivers because the call works on null.'),
        $t('When is a struct a good design? Discuss readonly struct, the in modifier, defensive copies and why large mutable structs are dangerous.',
            'Small immutable structs avoid heap allocations and improve locality. A mutable struct stored in a readonly field or returned from a property gets copied, so mutations are silently lost; large structs are expensive to copy on every call. readonly struct lets the compiler skip defensive copies, and in passes by reference without allowing modification, useful for hot paths.'),
    ],

    'backend-csharp_dotnet-s2-set2' => [
        $t('Why is async void dangerous? Explain how calling .Result or .Wait() on a Task can deadlock in a UI or legacy ASP.NET context and how ConfigureAwait(false) relates to it.',
            'async void cannot be awaited and its exceptions are raised on the synchronisation context and can crash the process; use it only for event handlers. Blocking on a Task while the awaited continuation needs the same single-threaded context (UI thread or classic ASP.NET request context) deadlocks. ConfigureAwait(false) in library code avoids resuming on the captured context, but the real fix is to be async all the way instead of blocking.'),
        $t('Compare awaiting several tasks one after another with Task.WhenAll. How are exceptions and cancellation handled, and what is the effect on total latency?',
            'Sequential awaits take the sum of the durations, WhenAll starts everything and completes in about the time of the slowest. WhenAll surfaces the first exception when awaited (all are available on the Task.Exception AggregateException), so inspect the individual tasks if you need every failure. Pass one CancellationToken to all work and observe it so cancellation stops everything promptly; limit concurrency when starting thousands of tasks.'),
        $t('What are IAsyncEnumerable<T> and await foreach, and when do they beat returning a List<T>? How does yield return work behind the scenes?',
            'IAsyncEnumerable streams results asynchronously one item at a time, so large result sets or slow producers do not need to be buffered fully in memory, and the consumer can start early. yield return makes the compiler generate a state machine that resumes after each item. Remember to support cancellation with [EnumeratorCancellation] and to dispose of the underlying resources.'),
        $t('Explain the difference between CPU-bound and I/O-bound work. When is Task.Run appropriate, and why is wrapping I/O in Task.Run inside an ASP.NET Core request a mistake?',
            'I/O-bound work should use true async APIs, freeing the thread while waiting. CPU-bound work occupies a thread, so in a desktop app Task.Run offloads it from the UI thread. In ASP.NET Core a request already runs on a thread-pool thread, so Task.Run only adds a hop and pool pressure without benefit; use async I/O and, for heavy CPU work, a background queue or separate worker.'),
        $t('A LINQ query over an in-memory list of 100,000 items is slow. Which patterns cause poor performance (multiple enumeration, Count() > 0, nested loops via Where inside Select) and how would you fix them?',
            'Common problems: enumerating the same deferred query repeatedly, using Count() > 0 instead of Any(), nested Where lookups that are O(n squared) instead of building a Dictionary or ToLookup first, sorting repeatedly, and allocating many intermediate objects. Fix by materialising once, using hash-based lookups, choosing GroupBy or Join appropriately and measuring with BenchmarkDotNet before optimising.'),
    ],

    'backend-csharp_dotnet-s2-set3' => [
        $t('Compare workstation and server garbage collection, concurrent GC and why calling GC.Collect() is usually wrong. Which tools show GC behaviour in a running app?',
            'Workstation GC favours responsiveness on a single heap; server GC uses a heap and thread per core for throughput, with higher memory use. Background (concurrent) GC reduces gen 2 pauses. Forcing collections disrupts the generational tuning and usually hurts performance. Use dotnet-counters, dotnet-trace, PerfView and runtime metrics (gen 0/1/2 counts, pause time, allocation rate) to observe.'),
        $t('What are tiered compilation, ReadyToRun and Native AOT? What trade-offs do they bring for startup time, throughput and compatibility?',
            'Tiered compilation first JITs quickly with light optimisation and re-JITs hot methods with full optimisation. ReadyToRun precompiles IL to native code to cut startup but still allows JIT. Native AOT compiles to a self-contained native binary with the fastest startup and smallest footprint, but restricts runtime reflection, dynamic code generation and some libraries, so it needs trimming-safe code.'),
        $t('How do Span<T>, Memory<T> and ArrayPool<T> reduce allocations? Give an example where you would use them and a limitation of Span<T>.',
            'Span<T> is a stack-only view over contiguous memory (arrays, stackalloc, strings) allowing slicing without copying, ideal for parsing; Memory<T> is the heap-storable counterpart for async code; ArrayPool rents and returns buffers to avoid repeated large allocations. Span is a ref struct, so it cannot be a field of a class, be captured in lambdas or live across await.'),
        $t('Reflection is flexible but slow. How do attributes, expression trees, compiled delegates and source generators help you get the flexibility without the runtime cost?',
            'Reflection lookups and invocations are slower and block trimming and AOT. Caching MemberInfo, building compiled delegates from expression trees, or using source generators (for System.Text.Json, logging, regex, DI) moves the work to compile time and produces plain fast code. Attributes provide metadata that generators and frameworks consume.'),
        $t('How do NuGet package versioning, transitive dependencies and assembly binding cause problems such as version conflicts in a large solution, and how do you manage them?',
            'Different projects may pull different versions of the same package transitively, and the highest version generally wins, which can break a library built against an older API. Manage it with central package management (Directory.Packages.props), lock files, consistent target frameworks, semantic versioning discipline and CI checks for vulnerabilities and conflicts. Older .NET Framework projects used binding redirects for the same problem.'),
    ],

    // ───────────────────────── Series 3 — high-bar scenarios ─────────────────────────

    'backend-csharp_dotnet-s3-set1' => [
        $t('Design a Money value object in C# for a payments system. What should it contain, how do you handle equality, rounding and currency mismatches, and why decimal rather than double?',
            'An immutable record struct or class holding Amount (decimal) and Currency, with value equality, validation in the constructor and operations (Add, Subtract) that reject mismatched currencies. decimal is base-10 with exact representation of money values while double has binary rounding errors. Define a rounding policy (banker rounding versus away-from-zero) and keep minor units explicit when storing or serialising.', 'architecture'),
        $t('How would you implement a thread-safe lazy singleton in C#? Compare Lazy<T>, static initialisation and double-checked locking, and say when a singleton is a bad idea.',
            'Static readonly initialisation or Lazy<T> (thread-safe by default with LazyThreadSafetyMode.ExecutionAndPublication) are the simple correct options; hand-written double-checked locking needs volatile and is easy to get wrong. Singletons hide dependencies and make testing hard; prefer dependency injection with a singleton lifetime and keep the type stateless or thread-safe.'),
        $t('Show how you would implement IDisposable and IAsyncDisposable correctly for a class that owns both managed and unmanaged resources. When is a finalizer needed?',
            'Use the dispose pattern: Dispose() calls Dispose(true) and GC.SuppressFinalize(this); Dispose(bool disposing) releases managed disposables only when disposing is true and unmanaged resources always; make it idempotent. Add a finalizer only if the class directly owns unmanaged handles (preferably wrapped in SafeHandle). Implement IAsyncDisposable with DisposeAsyncCore for asynchronous cleanup and use await using.'),
        $t('Should a method signal expected failures with exceptions or with a result type such as Result<T>? Discuss performance, readability and where each fits in a layered application.',
            'Exceptions suit truly exceptional, unexpected conditions and cross layers conveniently, but throwing is slow and hides failure paths in signatures. A Result or OneOf type makes expected outcomes (validation failed, not found) explicit and cheap, at the cost of more plumbing. A common rule: results for business outcomes inside the domain and application layers, exceptions for programmer errors and infrastructure faults handled at a boundary.', 'architecture'),
        $t('Review this code: foreach (var order in orders) { Task.Run(() => Process(order)); } return Ok(); What problems do you see?',
            'It is fire-and-forget: exceptions are unobserved and lost, the response returns before work finishes, there is no cancellation or throttling, and starting one task per order can flood the thread pool. Process may also capture loop state incorrectly in older C# versions. Use a background queue (Channel or hosted service) with limited concurrency, or await Task.WhenAll with a bounded degree of parallelism and proper error handling.', 'scenario'),
    ],

    'backend-csharp_dotnet-s3-set2' => [
        $t('Design a producer-consumer pipeline in .NET with System.Threading.Channels. How do bounded channels give backpressure, and how would you shut it down gracefully?',
            'Producers write to a Channel<T>; consumers read with ReadAllAsync. A bounded channel with a capacity makes WriteAsync wait (or drop, depending on BoundedChannelFullMode) when consumers are slower, preventing unbounded memory growth. For shutdown, call Writer.Complete() so consumers drain and finish, and use a CancellationToken for forced stop, awaiting all consumer tasks.', 'architecture'),
        $t('A legacy service randomly hangs under load because of sync-over-async code (.Result on Tasks). How do you diagnose it and what is the long-term fix?',
            'Symptoms: thread pool starvation, requests queued with low CPU. Diagnose with dotnet-dump or dotnet-stack to see many threads blocked in Task.Wait or GetResult, and dotnet-counters for thread pool queue length. The durable fix is to make the call chain async end to end; short-term mitigations are increasing minimum threads, ConfigureAwait(false) in libraries and removing blocking hot spots.', 'scenario'),
        $t('Why can you not await inside a lock statement, and how do you limit concurrent async operations with SemaphoreSlim?',
            'lock uses Monitor, which is thread-affine and cannot be held across an await because the continuation may run on a different thread; the compiler forbids it. SemaphoreSlim.WaitAsync lets you throttle async work (for example at most 5 concurrent HTTP calls) and must be released in a finally block.'),
        $t('A Dictionary shared by several threads occasionally throws or loops forever. Explain why, and what ConcurrentDictionary guarantees and does not guarantee (for example about GetOrAdd).',
            'Dictionary is not thread-safe; concurrent writes can corrupt its internal buckets, causing exceptions or infinite loops. ConcurrentDictionary makes individual operations atomic, but the value factory in GetOrAdd or AddOrUpdate may run more than once and its result is discarded when another thread wins, so factories must be side-effect free or wrapped in Lazy<T>.'),
        $t('How do you design cancellation and timeouts in a chain of async calls? Explain CancellationToken propagation, CancellationTokenSource.CreateLinkedTokenSource and cleanup.',
            'Accept a CancellationToken in every async method and pass it down to I/O calls; check it in loops. Combine a caller token with a timeout using CreateLinkedTokenSource(token) plus CancelAfter, and dispose the source. Treat OperationCanceledException as an expected outcome, not an error, and make cleanup idempotent so partially completed work leaves a consistent state.'),
    ],

    'backend-csharp_dotnet-s3-set3' => [
        $t('A .NET service in production shows steadily rising memory and occasional high CPU. Describe your step-by-step diagnosis using metrics, dumps and traces.',
            'Start with runtime metrics (dotnet-counters: GC heap size, gen 2 collections, allocation rate, thread pool queue length), reproduce under load, capture dotnet-gcdump or a dump and compare snapshots to find types growing and their roots, use dotnet-trace or a profiler for CPU hot paths, then fix and verify with the same measurements. Check for event handler leaks, static caches, undisposed objects and unbounded queues.', 'scenario'),
        $t('A service with millions of small long-lived objects suffers long GC pauses. What can you do at the design and configuration level?',
            'Reduce allocations and object counts (structs or arrays of structs, pooling, flattening object graphs), avoid keeping huge object graphs in gen 2, consider server and concurrent GC settings, use GC settings such as conserve memory or latency modes where appropriate, and move large caches out of process. Measure pause times before and after; do not tune blindly.', 'architecture'),
        $t('Plan an upgrade of a large application from .NET 6 to a current LTS version with minimal risk. What do you check and how do you roll it out?',
            'Review the breaking-change and deprecation notes, update the SDK and global.json, run the .NET Upgrade Assistant or analyzers, update packages and test dependencies, enable warnings as errors for obsolete APIs, run the full test suite and load tests, deploy to staging with canary or blue-green releases and watch metrics. Prefer LTS releases and plan the next upgrade before support ends.', 'architecture'),
        $t('What happens when a background Task throws an exception that nobody observes, and how should you structure background work so failures are not silently lost?',
            'Since .NET 4.5 unobserved task exceptions no longer crash the process by default, they are raised through TaskScheduler.UnobservedTaskException when the task is finalised, so failures can vanish. Await tasks, wrap background loops in try/catch with logging, use BackgroundService with a clear failure policy, and monitor with health checks and alerts.', 'situational'),
        $t('Design logging and observability for a .NET microservice: structured logs, correlation IDs, metrics and traces. How do you avoid leaking personal data?',
            'Use structured logging (ILogger with message templates, Serilog or built-in providers) with consistent properties, propagate a correlation or trace ID via W3C traceparent, export traces, metrics and logs with OpenTelemetry, and alert on SLO-based signals. Redact or never log secrets and personal data, use log levels and sampling to control volume, and make logs searchable by trace ID.', 'architecture'),
    ],
];
